, ${\\mathbf{a}}_{2}$, ${\\mathbf{a}}_{3}$ and ${\\mathbf{a}}_{4}$ be the respective columns of $A$.\nDetermine if $\\mathcal{B}=\\left\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}\\right\\}$ is a basis of $\\operatorname{Col}(A)$.\nIf that is the case, find $x_{1} \\cdot x_{2}$, where ${\\mathbf{a}}_{4}={x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}$.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 10 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186184761 "Let $W$ be the set of all polynomials in the variable $x\\in \\mathbb{R}$ with coefficients in $\\mathbb{R}$ of degree $5$ or greater. Complete the following sentence:\n\n*$W$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to polynomials addition and multiplication of a polynomial by a real number) because $\\text{_________}\\,.$*"] [17592186184762 "From top to bottom, fill in the blanks and hence complete the proof of the following statement using the axioms $[\\text{A}1]-[\\text{A}8]$ given below.\n\n*Statement:* \n\n*$\\alpha \\mathbf{0}=\\mathbf{0}$ for any $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{R}$*\n\n*Proof:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha \\mathbf{0} & =\\alpha \\mathbf{0} &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{given}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ \\alpha \\mathbf{0} & =\\alpha (\\mathbf{0}+\\mathbf{0}) &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{axiom A3}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ \\alpha \\mathbf{0} & =\\alpha \\mathbf{0}+\\alpha \\mathbf{0} &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{axiom __}\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ \\alpha \\mathbf{0}+(-\\alpha \\mathbf{0}) & =(\\alpha \\mathbf{0}+\\alpha \\mathbf{0})+(-\\alpha \\mathbf{0}) &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{add }-\\alpha \\mathbf{0}\\text{ to both sides}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ \\alpha \\mathbf{0}+(-\\alpha \\mathbf{0}) & =\\alpha \\mathbf{0}+(\\alpha \\mathbf{0}+(-\\alpha \\mathbf{0})) &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{axiom A2}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ \\mathbf{0} & =\\alpha \\mathbf{0}+\\mathbf{0} &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{axiom A4}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ \\mathbf{0} & =\\alpha \\mathbf{0} &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{axiom __}\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe axioms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ll}{\\color{black}\\text{[A1]}}:{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{y}=\\mathbf{y}+\\mathbf{x}} & {\\color{black}\\text{[A5]}}:{\\color{blue}1\\cdot \\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{x}} \\\\ {\\color{black}\\text{[A2]}}:{\\color{blue}(\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{y})+\\mathbf{z}=\\mathbf{x}+(\\mathbf{y}+\\mathbf{z})} & {\\color{black}\\text{[A6]}}:{\\color{blue}\\alpha (\\beta \\mathbf{x})=(\\alpha \\beta )\\mathbf{x}} \\\\ {\\color{black}\\text{[A3]}}:{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{0}=\\mathbf{x}} & {\\color{black}\\text{[A7]}}:{\\color{blue}(\\alpha +\\beta )\\mathbf{x}=\\alpha \\mathbf{x}+\\beta \\mathbf{x}} \\\\ {\\color{black}\\text{[A4]}}:{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{x}+(-\\mathbf{x})=\\mathbf{0}} & {\\color{black}\\text{[A8]}}:{\\color{blue}\\alpha (\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{y})=\\alpha \\mathbf{x}+\\alpha \\mathbf{y}}\\end{array}\n$$"] [17592186184763 "Let $C$ be the set of functions $f:\\mathbb{R}\\to \\mathbb{R}$ such that $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in \\mathbb{R}.$ Complete the following sentence:\n\n*$C$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to function addition and scalar multiplication of a function by a real number) because $\\text{_________}\\,.$*"] [17592186184764 "From top to bottom, fill in the blanks and hence complete the proof of the following statement using the axioms $[\\text{A}1]-[\\text{A}8]$ given below.\n\n*Statement:*\n\n*If $\\mathbf{w}$ is a vector such that $\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{w}=\\mathbf{x}$ for some $\\mathbf{x}\\in V$, then $\\mathbf{w}=\\mathbf{0}.$*\n\n*Proof:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{w} & =\\mathbf{x} &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{given}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ (\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{w})+(-\\mathbf{x}) & =\\mathbf{x}+(-\\mathbf{x}) &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{add }-\\mathbf{x}\\text{ to both sides}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ (\\mathbf{w}+\\mathbf{x})+(-\\mathbf{x}) & =\\mathbf{x}+(-\\mathbf{x}) &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{axiom __}\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ \\mathbf{w}+(\\mathbf{x}+(-\\mathbf{x})) & =\\mathbf{x}+(-\\mathbf{x}) &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{axiom A2}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ \\mathbf{w}+\\mathbf{0} & =\\mathbf{0} &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{axiom A4}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ \\mathbf{w} & =\\mathbf{0} &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{axiom __}\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe axioms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ll}{\\color{black}\\text{[A1]}}:{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{y}=\\mathbf{y}+\\mathbf{x}} & {\\color{black}\\text{[A5]}}:{\\color{blue}1\\cdot \\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{x}} \\\\ {\\color{black}\\text{[A2]}}:{\\color{blue}(\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{y})+\\mathbf{z}=\\mathbf{x}+(\\mathbf{y}+\\mathbf{z})} & {\\color{black}\\text{[A6]}}:{\\color{blue}\\alpha (\\beta \\mathbf{x})=(\\alpha \\beta )\\mathbf{x}} \\\\ {\\color{black}\\text{[A3]}}:{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{0}=\\mathbf{x}} & {\\color{black}\\text{[A7]}}:{\\color{blue}(\\alpha +\\beta )\\mathbf{x}=\\alpha \\mathbf{x}+\\beta \\mathbf{x}} \\\\ {\\color{black}\\text{[A4]}}:{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{x}+(-\\mathbf{x})=\\mathbf{0}} & {\\color{black}\\text{[A8]}}:{\\color{blue}\\alpha (\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{y})=\\alpha \\mathbf{x}+\\alpha \\mathbf{y}}\\end{array}\n$$"] [17592186192505 "Consider the subsets $\\; \\begin{gathered}A=\\left\\{\\begin{gathered} \\langle x, y, z \\rangle \\in \\mathbb{R}^3 :\\\\ x^2=y \\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}\\;$ and $\\; \\begin{gathered}B=\\\\\\left\\{\\begin{gathered} \\langle 3-t, -3+t, -6+2t \\rangle :\\\\ t \\in \\mathbb{R} \\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}.\\;$ Which of them is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3?$"] [17592186198774 "![](images/5c/5c4ffbb64a754df9a6100fda3fd6eaf4a68f28ac59de4dfb716c2c8b1f2847ab.png)\n\nUse set builder notation to describe the shaded region given in the diagram above:\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {{field-1}}\\,:\\,y\\,{{field-2}}\\,{x}^{2}\\}\n$$"] [17592186198777 "![](images/f0/f00499aad7b915267fe99bde8c13665a325cf6409e2e856466e9cdc90e4a8b8a.png)\n\nDescribe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation.\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {{field-1}}\\,:\\,y\\,{{field-2}}\\,2x-3\\}\n$$"] [17592186198805 "![](images/cc/cce16725dddcfc83098ee2323576b8e01bbc3f2625d17cb7e6030826ad9fbcd8.png)\n\nDescribe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation.\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {{field-1}}\\,:\\,y\\,{{field-2}}\\,x-1\\}\n$$"] [17592186204221 "Find $(3a - 2b) \\times c$, if $a \\times c = 2i - 3j$ and $b \\times c = 5i - 3j$."] [17592186204222 "Find $(a - 2b) \\times (4c)$, if $a \\times c = ⟨3, 2, 0⟩$ and $b \\times c = ⟨2, 3, 0⟩$."] [17592186204223 "Find $(5a - 3b) \\times (6b)$, if $b \\times a = ⟨1,-2, 3⟩$."] [17592186240977 "Let $\\mathbf{a}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find $\\mathbf{a}+\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{b}.$"] [17592186240979 "Let $\\mathbf{r}=\\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\mathbf{s}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ What is $(\\frac{1}{3}\\mathbf{r})\\cdot \\mathbf{s}?$"] [17592186240981 "Let\n$\\mathbf{p}=\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ y-3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\mathbf{q}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}.$\nIf $\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q}=0$, find the value of $y.$"] [17592186240982 "Let $\\mathbf{u}=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 3t \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\mathbf{v}=\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ {t}^{2} \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.$\nIf $\\mathbf{u}\\cdot \\mathbf{v}=-2$ and $t>3$, find the value of $t.$"] [17592186243958 "Let $F$ be the set of functions $f:\\mathbb{R}\\to \\mathbb{R}$ such that $f(0)=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Complete the following sentence:\n\n*$F$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to function addition and scalar multiplication of a function by a real number) because $\\text{_________}\\,.$*"] [17592186243984 "From top to bottom, fill in the blanks and hence complete the proof of the following statement using the axioms $[\\text{A}1]-[\\text{A}8]$ given below.\n\n*Statement:*\n\n*Given any $\\mathbf{x}\\in V$, if $\\mathbf{y}$ is a vector such that $\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{y}=\\mathbf{0}$, then $\\mathbf{y}=-\\mathbf{x}.$* \n\n*Proof:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{y} & =\\mathbf{0} &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{given}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ (\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{y})+(-\\mathbf{x}) & =\\mathbf{0}+(-\\mathbf{x}) &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{add }-\\mathbf{x}\\text{ to both sides}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ (\\mathbf{y}+\\mathbf{x})+(-\\mathbf{x}) & =(-\\mathbf{x})+\\mathbf{0} &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{axiom A1}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ (\\mathbf{y}+\\mathbf{x})+(-\\mathbf{x}) & =-\\mathbf{x} &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{axiom __}\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ \\mathbf{y}+(\\mathbf{x}+(-\\mathbf{x})) & =-\\mathbf{x} &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{axiom __}\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ \\mathbf{y}+\\mathbf{0} & =-\\mathbf{x} &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{axiom A4}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ \\mathbf{y} & =-\\mathbf{x} &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{axiom A3}\\hspace{0.222em}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe axioms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ll}{\\color{black}\\text{[A1]}}:{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{y}=\\mathbf{y}+\\mathbf{x}} & {\\color{black}\\text{[A5]}}:{\\color{blue}1\\cdot \\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{x}} \\\\ {\\color{black}\\text{[A2]}}:{\\color{blue}(\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{y})+\\mathbf{z}=\\mathbf{x}+(\\mathbf{y}+\\mathbf{z})} & {\\color{black}\\text{[A6]}}:{\\color{blue}\\alpha (\\beta \\mathbf{x})=(\\alpha \\beta )\\mathbf{x}} \\\\ {\\color{black}\\text{[A3]}}:{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{0}=\\mathbf{x}} & {\\color{black}\\text{[A7]}}:{\\color{blue}(\\alpha +\\beta )\\mathbf{x}=\\alpha \\mathbf{x}+\\beta \\mathbf{x}} \\\\ {\\color{black}\\text{[A4]}}:{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{x}+(-\\mathbf{x})=\\mathbf{0}} & {\\color{black}\\text{[A8]}}:{\\color{blue}\\alpha (\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{y})=\\alpha \\mathbf{x}+\\alpha \\mathbf{y}}\\end{array}\n$$"] [17592186243985 "From top to bottom, fill in the blanks and hence complete the proof of the following statement using the axioms $[\\text{A}1]-[\\text{A}8]$ given below.\n\n*Statement:*\n\n*If $\\mathbf{y}$ and $\\mathbf{z}$ are vectors such that $\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{y}=\\mathbf{z}+\\mathbf{x}$ for some $\\mathbf{x}\\in V$, then $\\mathbf{y}=\\mathbf{z}.$*\n\n*Proof:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{y} & =\\mathbf{z}+\\mathbf{x} &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{given}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ (\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{y})+(-\\mathbf{x}) & =(\\mathbf{z}+\\mathbf{x})+(-\\mathbf{x}) &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{add }-\\mathbf{x}\\text{ to both sides}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ (\\mathbf{y}+\\mathbf{x})+(-\\mathbf{x}) & =(\\mathbf{z}+\\mathbf{x})+(-\\mathbf{x}) &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{axiom A1}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ \\mathbf{y}+(\\mathbf{x}+(-\\mathbf{x})) & =\\mathbf{z}+(\\mathbf{x}+(-\\mathbf{x})) &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{axiom __}\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ \\mathbf{y}+\\mathbf{0} & =\\mathbf{z}+\\mathbf{0} &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{axiom A4}\\hspace{0.222em}] \\\\ \\mathbf{y} & =\\mathbf{z} &  & [\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{axiom __}\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe axioms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ll}{\\color{black}\\text{[A1]}}:{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{y}=\\mathbf{y}+\\mathbf{x}} & {\\color{black}\\text{[A5]}}:{\\color{blue}1\\cdot \\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{x}} \\\\ {\\color{black}\\text{[A2]}}:{\\color{blue}(\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{y})+\\mathbf{z}=\\mathbf{x}+(\\mathbf{y}+\\mathbf{z})} & {\\color{black}\\text{[A6]}}:{\\color{blue}\\alpha (\\beta \\mathbf{x})=(\\alpha \\beta )\\mathbf{x}} \\\\ {\\color{black}\\text{[A3]}}:{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{0}=\\mathbf{x}} & {\\color{black}\\text{[A7]}}:{\\color{blue}(\\alpha +\\beta )\\mathbf{x}=\\alpha \\mathbf{x}+\\beta \\mathbf{x}} \\\\ {\\color{black}\\text{[A4]}}:{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{x}+(-\\math